.TH RDATE 1M "4 November 2026" "rdate 1.11"
.SH NAME
rdate \- set the clock from a time server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /opt/amix/sbin/rdate
[
.B \-46anpsuv
] [
.B \-t
.I sec
] [
.B \-r
.I n
] [
.B \-b
.I sec
] [
.B \-d
.I msec
] [
.B \-o
.I port
]
.I host
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rdate
asks a time server for the time and sets the system clock from it.
With
.B \-n
it speaks SNTP (RFC 5905) to UDP port 123, which every public time
server answers; without it, the older RFC 868 time protocol to TCP port
37.
Each
.I host
is tried in turn, each of its addresses in turn, until one gives a usable
time.
.PP
Every try is bounded: the name lookup, the connection and the exchange
together get
.B \-t
seconds, after which the try is given up, so neither a silent network nor
a name server that does not answer can hold
.B rdate
up.
A host is tried
.B \-r
more times before the next one.
For one host the longest
.B rdate
can take is
.RI ( n "+1) \(mu " sec
seconds, and about one more.
.PP
The clock is stepped with
.BR settimeofday (3),
which sets it through
.BR stime (2)
to the whole second (a fraction of half a second or more rounds up), or,
with
.BR \-a ,
slewed with
.BR adjtime (2),
which runs the clock slightly fast or slow until the difference is gone.
Setting the clock needs root;
.B \-p
does not.
.PP
.B rdate
has no configuration file and runs no daemon.
The base system's
.BR ntpsync (1M)
runs it at boot and every hour, with the servers named in
.BR /etc/rc.conf .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Use SNTP.
The reply is checked: the server's mode, a version from 1 to 4, the
random cookie this request sent echoed back, a stratum from 1 to 14, a
leap indicator other than 3 (not synchronised), and offsets that agree
from one reply to the next.
A stratum of 0 is a kiss-o'-death, by which a server asks its clients to
stop or slow down: it is refused, not retried.
.TP
.BI \-t " sec"
The time allowed for each try, the name lookup included (default 15).
.TP
.BI \-r " n"
How many more times to try a host before the next one (default 1).
A name that does not resolve is not tried again.
.TP
.B \-a
Slew the clock with
.BR adjtime (2)
instead of stepping it, when the difference is at most
.B \-b
seconds (default 60 with
.BR \-a );
a bigger difference is stepped.
.TP
.BI \-b " sec"
Step the clock when the difference is more than
.I sec
seconds, slew it otherwise.
.B "\-b 0"
with
.B \-a
always slews.
.TP
.BI \-d " msec"
Leave the clock alone when the exchange took more than
.I msec
milliseconds.
.TP
.B \-p
Print the time and the difference, but do not set the clock.
.TP
.B \-s
Do not print the date.
.TP
.B \-v
Also print the difference applied, as described under OUTPUT.
.TP
.B \-u
Use UDP for the RFC 868 protocol.
.TP
.BI \-o " port"
Use
.I port
instead of 123 or 37.
.TP
.B \-4
Use IPv4 addresses, the only ones this system has.
.TP
.B \-6
Use IPv6 addresses: on this system the lookup fails.
.SH OUTPUT
On success,
.B rdate
prints the new date to the standard output, unless
.B \-s
is given, and, with
.BR \-v ,
.B \-p
or
.BR \-a ,
one line
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
rdate: adjust local clock by -12.482113 seconds (192.0.2.1)
.ft R
.fi
.PP
naming the difference and the address that answered.
When
.B \-b
decided, the line says
.B (adjtime)
or
.B (instant change)
before the address.
On a failure it prints one line to the standard error for each host that
gave no usable time, such as
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
rdate: 0.pool.ntp.org: no usable answer in 15 s (2 tries)
rdate: time.example: host not found
rdate: 192.0.2.1: refused: stratum 0 (kiss-o'-death RATE)
.ft R
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The clock was set, or with
.BR \-p ,
the time printed.
.TP
.B 1
Another failure: the connection was refused, no route, a socket error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.TP
.B 3
The name did not resolve.
.TP
.B 4
No usable answer within
.B \-t
seconds, on any try.
.TP
.B 5
The server's reply was refused (kiss-o'-death, not synchronised, times
that disagree).
.TP
.B 6
The exchange took longer than
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B 7
Setting the clock failed (not root, or the system refused).
.PP
With several hosts, the status is the last host's.
.SH EXAMPLES
What
.BR ntpsync (1M)
runs at boot, which steps the clock:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
/opt/amix/sbin/rdate -n -s -v -t 15 -r 1 0.pool.ntp.org 1.pool.ntp.org
.ft R
.fi
.PP
and every hour, which slews a small difference so that the clock never
runs backwards under
.BR cron (1M):
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
/opt/amix/sbin/rdate -n -a -s -v -t 15 -r 1 0.pool.ntp.org 1.pool.ntp.org
.ft R
.fi
.PP
To look without changing anything:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
rdate -n -p pool.ntp.org
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ntpsync (1M),
.BR date (1),
.BR adjtime (2),
.BR stime (2)
.SH NOTES
Names resolve as the system's resolver does: with DNS off (the default
of a stock system), only
.B /etc/hosts
is read, and a pool name fails at once with
.BR "host not found" .
.PP
The SNTP arithmetic keeps NTP's 64-bit fixed point and takes differences
modulo 2^64, so a clock up to 68 years wrong (such as one the battery
clock never set, in 1977) is set correctly, also across NTP's 2036 era
change.
The program has no floating point, so it runs on an FPU-less 68LC040 or
68LC060 without the kernel's emulator.
.PP
This kernel's
.BR adjtime (2)
slews at about one second per second: to gain, the clock runs about twice
as fast; to lose, it stands still until the difference is gone, and never
runs backwards.
A slew of 20 seconds takes about 20 seconds, during which a lost second
is a second in which the clock does not move; hourly differences are
fractions of a second.
.PP
A step is not recorded in
.B /var/adm/wtmp
as
.BR date (1)
records one.
